From: Daniel Ip <kg4368@yahoo.com.hk>
Date: Mon, 14 Jul 2026 14:20:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: qca8084: calibrate SerDes before reparenting port
 clocks

qca8084_qxgmii_set_mode() reparents the XPCS/MAC port clocks onto the
UNIPHY1 312.5 MHz SerDes output (qca8084_xpcs_clock_parent_set) BEFORE it
calibrates the SerDes (qca8084_pcs_set_mode -> qca8084_do_calibration).
The 312.5 MHz clock does not physically exist until calibration runs, so
on boards whose bootloader did not pre-calibrate the SerDes (e.g. the
consumer Linksys MBE7000, whose U-Boot does not init ethernet at all) the
clk_set_parent() triggers an RCG update that never completes:
"nss_cc_mac1_rx_clk_src: rcg didn't update its configuration" -> the four
QCA8084 PHYs fail probe with -16.

Reference boards (RDP / TP-Link BE800) tolerate the current order only
because their U-Boot already calibrated the SerDes. Calibration does not
depend on the clock reparenting, so performing it first is correct on all
boards and matches QSDK, which completes all SerDes calibration
(qca_mht_interface_mode_init) before configuring the port clocks
(ssdk_mht_gcc_clock_init). Just swap the two calls.

Signed-off-by: Daniel Ip <kg4368@yahoo.com.hk>
---
--- a/drivers/net/phy/qcom/qca8084_serdes.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/qcom/qca8084_serdes.c
@@ -622,11 +622,24 @@
 {
 	int ret;
 
-	ret = qca8084_xpcs_clock_parent_set(xpcs_mdiodev, pcs_mdiodev);
+	/*
+	 * Calibrate the SerDes (qca8084_pcs_set_mode -> qca8084_do_calibration,
+	 * which brings up the UNIPHY1 312.5 MHz output) BEFORE reparenting the
+	 * XPCS/MAC clocks onto it. qca8084_xpcs_clock_parent_set() switches the
+	 * mac*_rx/tx_clk_src RCGs to the 312.5 MHz SerDes root, which does not
+	 * physically exist until calibration has run - so on boards whose
+	 * bootloader did not pre-calibrate the SerDes (e.g. Linksys MBE7000) the
+	 * original order fails with "nss_cc_mac1_rx_clk_src: rcg didn't update".
+	 * Reference boards tolerate the reverse order only because U-Boot already
+	 * calibrated the SerDes. Calibration does not depend on the reparenting,
+	 * so doing it first is safe everywhere (and matches QSDK, which runs all
+	 * SerDes calibration before touching the port clocks).
+	 */
+	ret = qca8084_pcs_set_mode(xpcs_mdiodev, pcs_mdiodev);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
-	ret = qca8084_pcs_set_mode(xpcs_mdiodev, pcs_mdiodev);
+	ret = qca8084_xpcs_clock_parent_set(xpcs_mdiodev, pcs_mdiodev);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
